## Experiment A: free-delivery threshold ({{ promo.control_free_threshold }} -> {{ promo.treatment_free_threshold }})
Users: {{ sim.users }} randomised 50/50 on first visit between {{ start }} and {{ assign_end }}; each observed for {{ sim.observation_days }} days.

1. **Sample-ratio mismatch on one day.** On assignment day {{ srm.day }} ({{ srm_date }}), {{ (srm.lost_control_share * 100) | round | int }}% of control assignments were dropped from the assignment log ({{ ent.lost_control_assignments }} users). Those users still exist and still order; they are just missing from `assignments`. Expected signature: overall SRM; the daily split is balanced except on that day.
2. **Novelty effect.** Treatment lifts the order rate by {{ pe.order_lift_by_week | map("string") | join(", ") }} (relative) in weeks 1, 2 and 3 after assignment. Expected signature: a lift that decays week by week.
3. **Heterogeneous effect.** Users who signed up less than 90 days before the test get an extra {{ pe.new_user_extra_lift }} lift in every week. Platform and zone have no true effect differences.
4. **Cannibalisation of high-margin baskets.** In treatment, the probability that an order is a high-margin (small, top-up) basket is multiplied by {{ pe.high_margin_multiplier }}. Expected signature: lower high-margin share and lower product margin per order in treatment.
5. **Threshold bunching.** In treatment, {{ (pe.bunching_prob * 100) | round | int }}% of baskets within {{ pe.bunching_window }} below the new threshold are topped up to just above it.
- **Net truth:** more orders and GMV, lower contribution margin per user (lost fees + cheaper basket mix). The right call is not to launch at this threshold.

